Chapter: Rsa
  cites Foreword chapter CryptoBig
  cites Foreword chapter Sha256
  cites Foreword chapter Sha512

 RSA signature verification, PKCS#1 v1.5 with SHA-256. Verification
 only: there is no signing here and no private key anywhere in the
 chapter, because the only thing Codex needs from RSA is the ability to
 believe somebody else's certificate.

 THE SIGNATURE IS CHECKED BY CONSTRUCTION, NOT BY PARSING. The recovered
 block is compared against an encoded message built here from the
 message and the modulus width. The other way round -- recover, then
 walk the padding looking for the digest -- is how Bleichenbacher's 2006
 forgery worked: a lenient parser skips the padding it does not
 understand, and a signature with room left over can be forged against a
 small exponent without the private key. There is nothing to be lenient
 with if the expected bytes are built up front and compared whole.

 We say:

Section: The DigestInfo Prefix

 DER for `SEQUENCE { SEQUENCE { OID 2.16.840.1.101.3.4.2.1, NULL }, OCTET
 STRING (32) }` -- the SHA-256 DigestInfo header, with the digest itself
 appended after it. It is a constant because it is always the same
 nineteen bytes, and writing it as a constant is what lets the comparison
 above be a whole-block compare.

  rsa-sha256-prefix : List Integer =
    [48, 49, 48, 13, 6, 9, 96, 134, 72, 1, 101, 3, 4, 2, 1, 5, 0, 4, 32]

Section: The Digest

 `sha256` answers eight 32-bit WORDS, not thirty-two bytes. Feeding the
 word list somewhere a byte string is meant produces plausible output
 that is silently wrong, which is what once shipped a whole TLS key
 schedule in the wrong units. The conversion is big-endian and it happens
 here, once.

  rsa-digest-bytes : List Integer -> List Integer
  rsa-digest-bytes (msg) = rsa-words-bytes (sha256 msg) 0 []

  rsa-words-bytes : List Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  rsa-words-bytes (ws) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length ws then acc
    else rsa-words-bytes ws (i + 1) (rsa-push-word acc (list-at ws i))

  rsa-push-word : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  rsa-push-word (acc) (w) =
    let a1 = list-push acc (bit-and (bit-shru w 24) 255)
    in let a2 = list-push a1 (bit-and (bit-shru w 16) 255)
    in let a3 = list-push a2 (bit-and (bit-shru w 8) 255)
    in list-push a3 (bit-and w 255)

Section: SHA-384

 The same construction one digest wider. DigiCert Trusted Root G4 signs
 with sha384WithRSAEncryption, so without this that root is an anchor
 whose certificates could be parsed and never checked.

 SHA-384's words are 64 bits, not 32, so the byte conversion is its own
 function rather than the SHA-256 one reused -- eight bytes per word, big
 endian, six words, forty-eight bytes.

  rsa-sha384-prefix : List Integer =
    [48, 65, 48, 13, 6, 9, 96, 134, 72, 1, 101, 3, 4, 2, 2, 5, 0, 4, 48]

  rsa-digest384-bytes : List Integer -> List Integer
  rsa-digest384-bytes (msg) = rsa-words64-bytes (sha384 msg) 0 []

  rsa-words64-bytes : List Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  rsa-words64-bytes (ws) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length ws then acc
    else rsa-words64-bytes ws (i + 1) (rsa-push-word64 acc (list-at ws i))

  rsa-push-word64 : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  rsa-push-word64 (acc) (w) =
    let a1 = list-push acc (bit-and (bit-shru w 56) 255)
    in let a2 = list-push a1 (bit-and (bit-shru w 48) 255)
    in let a3 = list-push a2 (bit-and (bit-shru w 40) 255)
    in let a4 = list-push a3 (bit-and (bit-shru w 32) 255)
    in let a5 = list-push a4 (bit-and (bit-shru w 24) 255)
    in let a6 = list-push a5 (bit-and (bit-shru w 16) 255)
    in let a7 = list-push a6 (bit-and (bit-shru w 8) 255)
    in list-push a7 (bit-and w 255)

Section: The Encoded Message

 EM = 0x00 || 0x01 || PS || 0x00 || T, where T is the DigestInfo and PS
 is 0xFF repeated to fill the modulus width. RFC 8017 section 9.2
 requires at least eight bytes of padding; a modulus too small to leave
 room for them cannot carry this signature at all, and the answer to that
 is an empty encoding that no recovered block can equal, rather than a
 shorter padding that would verify.

  rsa-em-sha256 : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  rsa-em-sha256 (msg) (k) = rsa-em-build (rsa-sha256-prefix & rsa-digest-bytes msg) k

  rsa-em-sha384 : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  rsa-em-sha384 (msg) (k) = rsa-em-build (rsa-sha384-prefix & rsa-digest384-bytes msg) k

  rsa-em-build : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  rsa-em-build (t) (k) =
    if k - 3 - list-length t < 8 then []
    else [0, 1] & rsa-ff (k - 3 - list-length t) [] & [0] & t

  rsa-ff : Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  rsa-ff (n) (acc) =
    if n <= 0 then acc
    else rsa-ff (n - 1) (list-push acc 255)

Section: Verification

 The signature is an integer strictly narrower than the modulus, and a
 signature of a different width is refused before any arithmetic: a
 shorter one would be left-padded into a different number, and a longer
 one is not a signature under this key at all.

  rsa-verify-pkcs1-sha256 : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  rsa-verify-pkcs1-sha256 (n-bytes) (e-bytes) (msg) (sig) =
    if list-length sig /= list-length n-bytes then False
    else rsa-check (cb-mod-exp-bytes sig e-bytes n-bytes) (rsa-em-sha256 msg (list-length n-bytes))

  rsa-verify-pkcs1-sha384 : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  rsa-verify-pkcs1-sha384 (n-bytes) (e-bytes) (msg) (sig) =
    if list-length sig /= list-length n-bytes then False
    else rsa-check (cb-mod-exp-bytes sig e-bytes n-bytes) (rsa-em-sha384 msg (list-length n-bytes))

  rsa-check : List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  rsa-check (got) (want) =
    if list-length want == 0 then False
    else rsa-bytes-eq got want

  rsa-bytes-eq : List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  rsa-bytes-eq (a) (b) =
    if list-length a /= list-length b then False
    else rsa-bytes-loop a b 0

  rsa-bytes-loop : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  rsa-bytes-loop (a) (b) (i) =
    if i >= list-length a then True
    else if list-at a i /= list-at b i then False
    else rsa-bytes-loop a b (i + 1)

Section: RSASSA-PSS

 The other RSA signature scheme, RFC 8017 section 8.1. TLS 1.3 does not
 offer a choice about it: RFC 8446 section 4.4.3 says a CertificateVerify
 from an RSA certificate MUST use RSASSA-PSS, and rsa_pkcs1_sha256 is
 permitted only in the certificate chain, never in the handshake
 signature. So a TLS client that can do only PKCS#1 v1.5 cannot
 authenticate an RSA server at all, which is the gap this section closes.

 THIS ONE CANNOT BE CHECKED BY CONSTRUCTION, and that is the whole
 difference from the section above. A PSS signature carries a random salt,
 so the same key over the same message gives a different signature every
 time and there is no expected block to build and compare whole. The
 encoding has to be taken apart. Every step below is therefore a check
 that has to be written down and can be left out, where v1.5 got them all
 from a single comparison -- so the order here follows RFC 8017 section
 9.1.2 step for step rather than being rearranged for readability.

 VERIFICATION ONLY, again. No salt is ever generated here, and nothing in
 this chapter needs a random number, which is worth noticing: PSS SIGNING
 needs a good one and gets weaker without it, and a verifier that shares
 code with a signer is a verifier that has an entropy dependency it does
 not need.

  rsa-pss-hlen : Integer = 32

 The salt is required to be exactly the digest length. RFC 8017 permits
 other lengths and even permits recovering the length from the encoding,
 and we do neither: rsa_pss_rsae_sha256 fixes it at 32, and a verifier
 that accepts whatever salt length it happens to find will accept a
 zero-length salt, which is a different scheme with a different security
 argument. Anything else is refused.

  rsa-pss-slen : Integer = 32

  rsa-pss-trailer : Integer = #BC

Section: MGF1

 The mask generation function, RFC 8017 appendix B.2.1. It is a hash in a
 counter loop: Hash(seed || 0, 1, 2, ...) concatenated until there are
 enough bytes, then truncated.

 The counter is four bytes big-endian, and it is I2OSP of an integer, not
 a byte. A three-byte counter, or a native-order one, produces a mask that
 is wrong from the first block and a verifier that rejects every genuine
 signature -- which reads as "PSS does not work" rather than as one wrong
 constant.

  rsa-mgf1 : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  rsa-mgf1 (seed) (want) = rsa-mgf1-loop seed want 0 []

  rsa-mgf1-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  rsa-mgf1-loop (seed) (want) (c) (acc) =
    if list-length acc >= want then rsa-take acc want
    else rsa-mgf1-loop seed want (c + 1) (acc & rsa-digest-bytes (seed & rsa-counter c))

  rsa-counter : Integer -> List Integer
  rsa-counter (c) =
    [bit-and (bit-shru c 24) 255, bit-and (bit-shru c 16) 255,
     bit-and (bit-shru c 8) 255, bit-and c 255]

Section: The Modulus Width In Bits

 emBits is modBits - 1, and modBits is the bit length of the modulus --
 not eight times its byte length. For every RSA key in ordinary use those
 are the same number, because the top bit of the modulus is set; but
 taking the byte length as the bit length is an assumption about somebody
 else's key material, and the one free bit it gets wrong is exactly the
 bit step 6 below checks.

  rsa-mod-bits : List Integer -> Integer
  rsa-mod-bits (n) =
    if list-length n < 1 then 0
    else (list-length n - 1) * 8 + rsa-top-bits (list-at n 0)

  rsa-top-bits : Integer -> Integer
  rsa-top-bits (b) = if b <= 0 then 0 else 1 + rsa-top-bits (bit-shru b 1)

Section: EMSA-PSS-VERIFY

 RFC 8017 section 9.1.2, in its order.

  rsa-verify-pss-sha256 : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  rsa-verify-pss-sha256 (n-bytes) (e-bytes) (msg) (sig) =
    if list-length sig /= list-length n-bytes then False
    else if list-length n-bytes < 1 then False
    else rsa-pss-em msg (cb-mod-exp-bytes sig e-bytes n-bytes) (rsa-mod-bits n-bytes)

 emLen is ceil(emBits / 8), which is ceil((modBits - 1) / 8), which is
 (modBits + 6) shifted right three times. The recovered block is as wide
 as the modulus, so when emLen is narrower the leading bytes have to BE
 zero rather than merely be ignored -- a nonzero byte there is a recovered
 value too large for the encoding, and dropping it silently would accept
 it.

  rsa-pss-em : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  rsa-pss-em (msg) (m) (mod-bits) =
    let em-len = bit-shru (mod-bits + 6) 3
    in if em-len < rsa-pss-hlen + rsa-pss-slen + 2 then False
       else if em-len > list-length m then False
       else rsa-pss-lead msg m mod-bits em-len (list-length m - em-len)

  rsa-pss-lead : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  rsa-pss-lead (msg) (m) (mod-bits) (em-len) (skip) =
    if rsa-all-zero m 0 skip == False then False
    else rsa-pss-trail msg (rsa-slice m skip em-len) mod-bits em-len

 Step 4: the last byte is 0xBC. Step 5 splits the rest into the masked
 data block and H.

  rsa-pss-trail : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  rsa-pss-trail (msg) (em) (mod-bits) (em-len) =
    if list-at em (em-len - 1) /= rsa-pss-trailer then False
    else rsa-pss-bits msg em em-len (em-len - rsa-pss-hlen - 1) (8 * em-len - (mod-bits - 1))

 Step 6, and it is the one that looks like bookkeeping and is not. The
 encoded message is emBits wide, not a whole number of bytes, so the top
 few bits of the first byte are not part of it and MUST be zero. A
 verifier that skips this accepts a family of distinct encodings of the
 same signature.

  rsa-pss-bits : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  rsa-pss-bits (msg) (em) (em-len) (db-len) (free) =
    if db-len < rsa-pss-slen + 1 then False
    else if bit-and (list-at em 0) (rsa-pss-top-mask free) /= 0 then False
    else rsa-pss-unmask msg (rsa-slice em 0 db-len) (rsa-slice em db-len rsa-pss-hlen) db-len free

 The top `free` bits set, as a byte: 256 minus 256 shifted right that far.

  rsa-pss-top-mask : Integer -> Integer
  rsa-pss-top-mask (free) = 256 - bit-shru 256 free

 Steps 7 and 8: unmask the data block with MGF1 seeded by H.

  rsa-pss-unmask : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  rsa-pss-unmask (msg) (masked) (h) (db-len) (free) =
    rsa-pss-db msg h (rsa-xor masked (rsa-mgf1 h db-len) 0 []) db-len free

 Step 9 clears the same top bits in the unmasked block, and then step 10
 is the padding check: everything before the salt is zero except for a
 single 0x01 immediately in front of it. The clearing happens BEFORE the
 zero check rather than being folded into it, because when the block is at
 its minimum width those top bits and the 0x01 separator are the same
 byte, and a check that assumed otherwise would reject a legal encoding.

  rsa-pss-db : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  rsa-pss-db (msg) (h) (raw) (db-len) (free) =
    let db = list-set-at raw 0 (bit-and (list-at raw 0) (bit-shru 255 free))
    in rsa-pss-pad msg h db (db-len - rsa-pss-slen - 1) db-len

  rsa-pss-pad : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  rsa-pss-pad (msg) (h) (db) (ps) (db-len) =
    if rsa-all-zero db 0 ps == False then False
    else if list-at db ps /= 1 then False
    else rsa-pss-final msg h (rsa-slice db (db-len - rsa-pss-slen) rsa-pss-slen)

 Steps 12 to 14. M' is EIGHT zero bytes, then the message digest, then the
 salt -- and the eight zeros are not padding to a boundary, they are a
 domain separator that stops H' ever being the hash of a bare message.
 Leaving them out still round-trips against a signer that also leaves them
 out, which is the failure this tree has already had once in the TLS key
 schedule.

  rsa-pss-final : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  rsa-pss-final (msg) (h) (salt) =
    rsa-bytes-eq h (rsa-digest-bytes (rsa-zeros 8 [] & rsa-digest-bytes msg & salt))

Section: PSS Helpers

  rsa-zeros : Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  rsa-zeros (n) (acc) =
    if n <= 0 then acc
    else rsa-zeros (n - 1) (list-push acc 0)

  rsa-take : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  rsa-take (bs) (n) = rsa-slice bs 0 n

  rsa-slice : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  rsa-slice (bs) (off) (n) = rsa-slice-loop bs off n 0 []

  rsa-slice-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  rsa-slice-loop (bs) (off) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if off + i >= list-length bs then acc
    else rsa-slice-loop bs off n (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at bs (off + i)))

  rsa-xor : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  rsa-xor (a) (b) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length a then acc
    else if i >= list-length b then acc
    else rsa-xor a b (i + 1) (list-push acc (bit-xor (list-at a i) (list-at b i)))

  rsa-all-zero : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  rsa-all-zero (bs) (from) (n) =
    if from >= n then True
    else if from >= list-length bs then False
    else if list-at bs from /= 0 then False
    else rsa-all-zero bs (from + 1) n

